      <t:profileDesc xmlns:t="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0">
         <t:creation/>
         <t:abstract>
            <t:p>According to his Gadl, the monk <t:persName ref="https://betamasaheft.eu/PRS1906Ananyao">ʾAnānyā</t:persName> came from the 
               <t:placeName ref="https://betamasaheft.eu/LOC4564Maragw">Marāgʷǝz district</t:placeName> of the province <t:placeName ref="https://betamasaheft.eu/LOC5575Saraye">Sarāye</t:placeName>. 
               He was a member of the monastic movement known as Ewostateans, following the guidance and doctrines of 
               <t:persName ref="https://betamasaheft.eu/PRS3934Ewostate">ʾEwosṭātewos</t:persName>. The birthplace of the latter in <t:placeName ref="https://betamasaheft.eu/LOC2358DabraS">Dabra Ṣarābi</t:placeName> is noted in the Gadl as 
               well as ʾEwosṭātewos’ successors <t:persName ref="https://betamasaheft.eu/PRS2385Bakimos">Bäkimos of Däbrä Ṣärabi</t:persName> and <t:persName ref="https://betamasaheft.eu/PRS7769Pawlos">Ṗawlos</t:persName>. ʾAnānyā travelled extensively. 
               He interceded with <t:persName ref="https://betamasaheft.eu/PRS7769Pawlos">Ṗawlos</t:persName> in  <t:placeName ref="https://betamasaheft.eu/LOC2358DabraS">Dabra Ṣarābi</t:placeName> to organise the defence of 
               Ethiopia against Muslim invaders. He succeeded <t:persName ref="https://betamasaheft.eu/PRS7769Pawlos">Ṗawlos</t:persName>. One day, he went to 
               <t:placeName ref="https://betamasaheft.eu/LOC1937Bizan">Bizän</t:placeName>, where he miraculously arrived in one hour instead of taking ten days as usual. Another time, 
               he went to the king and crossed the <t:placeName ref="https://betamasaheft.eu/LOC5864Takkaz">Takkaze</t:placeName> by parting the waters. According to the description, he
               died at the hands of an <t:persName ref="https://betamasaheft.eu/ETH1973Oromo">Oromo person</t:persName> in the land of 
               <t:placeName ref="https://betamasaheft.eu/LOC6408Zaeta">Zaʾǝta</t:placeName>. After his death, half of his disciples remained to watch over his body while the other half 
               went to <t:placeName ref="https://betamasaheft.eu/LOC5191Qata">Qata</t:placeName> to announce the event. The inhabitants of <t:placeName ref="https://betamasaheft.eu/LOC5191Qata">Qata</t:placeName> 
               quarrelled with those of <t:placeName ref="https://betamasaheft.eu/LOC4545Maqala">Maq̲ala</t:placeName> over the casket of ʾAnānyā. The cries of this quarrel could be heard
               in <t:placeName ref="https://betamasaheft.eu/LOC2947Endart">ʾƎndartā</t:placeName> and in <t:placeName ref="https://betamasaheft.eu/LOC5685Sera">Ṣǝraʿ</t:placeName>. He was buried in 
               <t:placeName ref="https://betamasaheft.eu/LOC5191Qata">Qata</t:placeName> on 11th Maskaram, but <t:persName ref="https://betamasaheft.eu/PRS5643Iyosayas">Iyosäyas</t:persName> and his other disciples decided 
               to remove his remains and transfer them to Dabra Ṣarābi, the monastery where he was to rest.</t:p>
         </t:abstract>
